<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    OWASP dependency-check suppressions.

    A suppression hides a finding, so a wrong one hides a real vulnerability.
    Every entry here names one package and one CVE, and carries the evidence
    that made it a false positive. A blanket CVE suppression, or a suppression
    with no stated evidence, does not belong in this file.

    Each entry answers one question: why is this finding about a product this
    repository does not use.

    See CHAT-icgifzbv.
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">

    <!--
        chat-persistence-xstream is our own module. It is named for the Redis
        Streams backend it implements, and it has nothing to do with the
        XStream XML library.

        Measured on 2026-09-21 at master d09a2daf: the module declares no
        XStream dependency, and none resolves in its tree.

        dependency-check matched our module artifactId against the XStream
        product CPE, and read our version 0.0.1 as XStream 0.0.1. Every CVE
        below is a real XStream defect and none of them reaches this code,
        because the library is absent.

        These are listed one CVE at a time rather than by CPE. A new XStream
        CVE will therefore fail the gate again and force a fresh reading.
        That is deliberate: this match is misidentification, and a standing
        CPE suppression would hide the day it stops being one.
    -->
    <suppress>
        <notes>Our own module, named for Redis Streams. No XStream library resolves.</notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/com\.demo/chat-persistence-xstream@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2013-7285</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2020-26217</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2021-21342</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2021-21344</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2021-21345</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2021-21346</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2021-21347</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2021-21350</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2021-21351</cve>
    </suppress>

    <!--
        CVE-2026-47837 is a flaw in the Spring Cloud Config Server /monitor
        webhook endpoint. That endpoint comes from spring-cloud-config-monitor
        and Spring Cloud Bus. It is not a flaw in the Consul configuration
        client.

        dependency-check matched spring-cloud-consul-config to the CPE
        vmware:spring_cloud_config, which is a different product.

        Measured on 2026-09-21 at master d09a2daf, and checked twice, once
        plainly and once with the register-consul and discovery-consul
        profiles active:
        - No pom declares spring-cloud-config-server, spring-cloud-config-monitor,
          spring-cloud-bus or spring-cloud-config-client.
        - None of those four resolves in any runtime tree of any module.
        - spring-cloud-starter-consul-config pulls only consul, commons,
          context and loadbalancer artifacts.
        - No source or yml names ConfigServer, config.monitor or
          MonitorController.

        So the vulnerable endpoint cannot exist in any deployment this
        repository builds. Recheck this entry if Config Server, Config Monitor
        or Bus is ever added.
    -->
    <suppress>
        <notes>Config Server /monitor endpoint flaw. This is the Consul config client, and no Config Server, Monitor or Bus resolves.</notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.springframework\.cloud/spring-cloud-consul-config@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2026-47837</cve>
    </suppress>

</suppressions>
